// winmm_enum_probe.nvgt - does anything in this plugin's winmm path kill the run?
//
// STATUS, run 37044062606: in from the case list, and it carries the pragma
// like every other case, so it does load the library. It is the one case of
// the eight that still dies - exit 0xC0000005 after 2.2s, 0 bytes of stdout,
// and a 5382-byte stderr byte for byte the same as the six cases that exit 0.
//
// What that stderr says is that this case never reached its own first line.
// ENUM_BEGIN is a print, and stdout holds nothing, so the death is before the
// script's main body runs: the engine spawned the process, the plugin's
// register_nvmidi ran to completion - "end of register_nvmidi (calls=183
// refused=0)" is in the log - and then the process died without executing a
// statement of this file.
//
// The stack cdb prints under that fault reads
//
//   winmmbase!midiOutGetNumDevs+0xb
//     <- InitDevices <- InternalLoadDriver <- winmmGetPrivateProfileString
//     <- RegGetValueW <- NtQueryValueKey
//
// and the nvmidi! frames printed beneath those (midi_export_backend_byte
// +0x11acf, midi_output_port_count+0xc2) are NOT a call stack: they are what
// cdb resolves a frame to when the frame's image has been unloaded, and the
// exception address itself says so - <Unloaded_nvmidi.dll>+0x15e6. Reading
// them as the plugin being on the stack is the mistake this header used to
// make.
//
// So the measurement is winmm's own device enumeration refusing, and the
// plugin is present only as the reason winmm is in the process at all. The
// next question is not which winmm call - it is whether the fault needs the
// plugin's code to have run, or only winmm's. Case n_handle2 answers the
// second half: it creates an output object, calls into it and exits 0, so
// building winmm's endpoint structures is not itself fatal.
//
// The pragma loads the library, and the question is what the library does
// with winmm, so the pragma is the point rather than boilerplate.
#pragma plugin nvmidi

void main() {
	print("ENUM_BEGIN");

	// Block one: enumeration only. Builds the process-wide RtMidiOut on first
	// use, calls its virtual getPortCount(), and leaves without opening
	// anything - so no MidiOutWinMM is ever constructed on this path.
	const uint outputs = midi_output_port_count();
	print("ENUM_OUTPUTS " + outputs);
	const uint inputs = midi_input_port_count();
	print("ENUM_INPUTS " + inputs);

	// The line above this one is the boundary. Everything below needs winmm
	// to have been brought in, and if the fault is in MidiOutWinMM it is here
	// that the process stops rather than after the earlier count.
	const uint api_bytes = midi_api_name_byte_count();
	print("ENUM_API_BYTES " + api_bytes);
	print("ENUM_MIDDLE");   // the boundary marker, read against the exit code

	// Block two: winmm. Constructed by creating the port object; the ctor
	// inside RtMidiOut is what picks MidiOutWinMM for this backend.
	midi_output@ port_out = midi_output_create();
	print("ENUM_CREATED");
	const bool opened = port_out.open(0, "probe");
	print("ENUM_OPENED " + opened);
	port_out.close();
	print("ENUM_CLOSED");

	print("ENUM_END");
}
